  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Given that $r(t)=\u27e87-3t,\\,2{t}^{2}\u27e9,$ what is $|{r}^{\u2032}(1)|?$\n\n$|{r}^{\u2032}(1)|=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We differentiate each component separately.\n\n- For the $x$-component $x(t)=7-3t,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(t)=-3,\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(1)=-3.\n$$\n\n- For the $y$-component $y(t)=2{t}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}(t)=4t,\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}(1)=4(1)=4.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\u2032}(1) & =\u27e8{x}^{\u2032}(1),\\,{y}^{\u2032}(1)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-3,\\,4\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the magnitude of this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{r}^{\u2032}(1)| & =\\sqrt{{(-3)}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
